.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Recharts"
.PP
\fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP
.SH "Introduction"
.PP
Recharts is a \fBRedefined\fP chart library built with \fRReact\fP and \fRD3\fP\&.

.PP
The main purpose of this library is to help you to write charts in React applications without any pain\&. Main principles of Recharts are:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
\fBSimply\fP deploy with React components\&.
.IP "2." 4
\fBNative\fP SVG support, lightweight depending only on some D3 submodules\&.
.IP "3." 4
\fBDeclarative\fP components, components of charts are purely presentational\&.
.PP

.PP
Documentation at \fRrecharts\&.org\fP and our \fRstorybook (WIP)\fP

.PP
Please see \fRthe wiki\fP for FAQ\&.

.PP
All development is done on the \fRmaster\fP branch\&. The current latest release and storybook documentation reflects what is on the \fRrelease\fP branch\&.
.SH "Examples"
.PP
.PP
.nf
<LineChart width={400} height={400} data={data} margin={{ top: 5, right: 20, left: 10, bottom: 5 }}>
  <XAxis dataKey="name" />
  <Tooltip />
  <CartesianGrid stroke="#f5f5f5" />
  <Line type="monotone" dataKey="uv" stroke="#ff7300" yAxisId={0} />
  <Line type="monotone" dataKey="pv" stroke="#387908" yAxisId={1} />
</LineChart>
.fi
.PP

.PP
All the components of Recharts are clearly separated\&. The lineChart is composed of x axis, tooltip, grid, and line items, and each of them is an independent React Component\&. The clear separation and composition of components is one of the principle Recharts follows\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
.SS "npm"
NPM is the easiest and fastest way to get started using Recharts\&. It is also the recommended installation method when building single-page applications (SPAs)\&. It pairs nicely with a CommonJS module bundler such as Webpack\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
# latest stable
$ npm install recharts
.fi
.PP
.SS "umd"
The UMD build is also available on unpkg\&.com:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script src="https://unpkg\&.com/react/umd/react\&.production\&.min\&.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg\&.com/react\-dom/umd/react\-dom\&.production\&.min\&.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg\&.com/recharts/umd/Recharts\&.min\&.js"></script>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Then you can find the library on \fRwindow\&.Recharts\fP\&.
.SS "dev build"
.PP
.nf
$ git clone https://github\&.com/recharts/recharts\&.git
$ cd recharts
$ npm install
$ npm run build
.fi
.PP
.SH "Demo"
.PP
To examine the demos in your local build, execute:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm run[\-script] demo
.fi
.PP

.PP
and then browse to http://localhost:3000\&.
.SH "Storybook"
.PP
We are in the process of unifying documentation and examples in storybook\&. To run it locally, execute

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm run[\-script] storybook
.fi
.PP

.PP
and then browse to http://localhost:6006\&.
.SH "Releases"
.PP
\fRReleases\fP are automated via GH Actions - when a new release is created in GH, CI will trigger that:

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Runs a build
.IP "2." 4
Runs tests
.IP "3." 4
Runs \fRnpm publish\fP
.PP

.PP
Version increments and tagging are not automated at this time\&.
.SS "Release testing"
Until we can automate more, it should be preferred to test as close to the results of \fRnpm publish\fP as we possibly can\&. This ensures we don't publish unintended breaking changes\&. One way to do that is using \fRyalc\fP - \fRnpm i -g yalc\fP\&.

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Make your changes in recharts
.IP "2." 4
\fRyalc publish\fP in recharts
.IP "3." 4
\fRyalc add recharts\fP in your test package (ex: in a vite or webpack reach app with recharts installed, imported, and your recent changes used)
.IP "4." 4
\fRnpm install\fP
.IP "5." 4
Test a local run, a build, etc\&.
.PP
.SH "Module Formats"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRbabel-plugin-recharts\fP A simple transform to cherry-pick Recharts modules so you don’t have to\&. \fBNote: this plugin is out of date and may not work with 2\&.x\fP
.PP
